Post-Surgical Pain (Chronic) (CPSP)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Surgical field pain >3 months, Neuropathic pain descriptors, Allodynia over scar territory

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-048
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, unrelenting, burning pain and extreme skin sensitivity over a 6-month-old surgical scar, completely preventing sleep and functional use of the limb.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient 6 months post-hernia repair experiencing a severe, paralyzing flare of neuropathic burning pain and allodynia mapped precisely to the surgical scar. Urgent assessment was required to definitively rule out a delayed, deep prosthetic mesh infection or recurrent hernia, and to initiate targeted, specialized neuropathic analgesia to halt the acute neuro-inflammatory crisis prior to daytime specialist review.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My hernia scar has been burning for six months, but tonight it feels like a blowtorch on my skin; I can't even let the bedsheets touch my stomach."
History of Present Illness: Patient is 6 months post-uncomplicated open inguinal hernia repair (with mesh). Presents with an acute-on-chronic pain crisis. Timeline: Persistent, debilitating pain localized to the original surgical field has lasted far longer than the expected healing timeframe (>3 months). The pain characteristics transformed 2 months ago into constant burning, tingling, and shooting neuropathic descriptors. Tonight, the patient is experiencing a severe flare of localized allodynia and hyperalgesia mapped precisely to the cutaneous distribution of the fully healed surgical scar and extending distally into the sensory territory of the ilioinguinal nerve. Pain intensity has progressively worsened despite full structural tissue healing. Sleep is entirely disrupted.
Functional Status: Bedbound, actively holding the abdomen exposed and refusing to allow any fabric to touch the skin.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound fatigue and secondary mood disturbance directly tied to the unyielding, chronic neuropathic pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 142/86 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, visibly exhausted, sitting rigidly to avoid any abdominal skin contact with clothing.
Physical Exam: Inspection of the right inguinal region reveals a completely mature, fully healed, flat surgical scar. Complete absence of any recurrent primary pathology (no palpable hernia defect or cough impulse) or signs of deep prosthetic mesh infection (no erythema, induration, focal heat, or purulent sinus tract). Severe cutaneous allodynia is vividly demonstrated: lightly brushing a cotton swab strictly over the scar and the immediate inferior dermatome (ilioinguinal nerve distribution) provokes a severe, flinching pain response. Hyperalgesia is present to pinprick testing in the same distribution. Abdomen is soft and non-tender away from the specific neuropathic field.
Environmental Observations: Patient’s bedroom is kept unnaturally cold to try and soothe the burning sensation.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking maximum doses of regular Paracetamol, Ibuprofen, and over-the-counter Codeine; completely inadequate for this neuropathic phenotype.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute flare of Chronic Post-Surgical Neuropathic Pain (Ilioinguinal Neuralgia) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Recurrent Inguinal Hernia - Ruled out entirely by negative clinical exam (no palpable defect or expansile cough impulse) and the purely neuropathic (burning/surface) quality of the pain rather than a deep dragging/bulging ache.

Deep Mesh Infection - Ruled out clinically by the completely clean, mature scar, absence of systemic fevers, and lack of localized induration or spreading cellulitis.

Complex Regional Pain Syndrome (CRPS) - Unlikely as the primary immediate diagnosis due to strictly confined single-nerve (ilioinguinal) distribution rather than regional spread with gross vasomotor changes, though it remains a long-term differential.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, immobilizing crisis of chronic post-surgical pain (CPSP), characterized by textbook neuropathic features (burning, allodynia) mapping precisely to a specific nerve frequently manipulated during hernia surgery. The critical clinical step was differentiating this severe nerve pain from a late surgical failure or deep mesh infection. Clinical examination definitively established the structural integrity of the repair and the absence of infection, confirming the pathology was entirely neurogenic. The severity of the allodynia rendered standard opiate and NSAID analgesia completely useless, leaving the patient in agony and unable to sleep. Urgent after-hours assessment safely provided diagnostic validation, prevented the inappropriate escalation of opioids, and initiated targeted membrane-stabilizing pharmacology to interrupt the aberrant nerve signaling.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Pregabalin 75mg STAT. Prescribed Pregabalin 75mg Nocté ongoing. Advised complete cessation of Codeine and NSAIDs, as they offer zero efficacy for this specific pain mechanism.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Apply a specialized, medical-grade lidocaine (numbing) patch directly over the healed scar before getting dressed in the morning. Consequence: The patch provides a physical barrier against clothing friction while locally numbing the hyperactive nerve endings, drastically reducing the constant burning and allowing for normal movement (positive reinforcement).
Action: Construct a physical "tent" over the groin using a blanket frame or pillows while in bed. Consequence: Complete elimination of unpredictable tactile stimulation from bedsheets, allowing for immediate psychological relief and sleep onset (negative reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a specialized Pain Medicine physician and chronic pain clinic via the regular GP.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to titrate neuropathic medications and expedite pain clinic referral.

